# Target C-II — Pareto profile for the small-leaf/center complete rectangle

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only phase-resolved structural sharpening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** corrected dual-tree-backbone rectangle theorem `target_c_small_leaf_complete_rectangle_t1844_2026-09-15.md`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected rectangle theorem fixes one block length, `0.003D`, and obtains a complete

\[
0.003D\times0.35D
\]

smallest-leaf/center crossing rectangle. The proof actually leaves the block length free. Keeping that parameter gives a full aspect-ratio tradeoff that can be chosen later to suit a strip-reuse or simultaneous-shear argument.

Put

\[
s_0={627\over42520}=0.01474600188\ldots,
\]

for the certified dual-tree backbone density,

\[
d_0={57\over100},
\qquad
n_0={1063\over1000},
\]

for the per-backbone-leaf center degree floor and total center-train ceiling, and

\[
\boxed{
\alpha:={n_0\over s_0}
={1129969\over15675}
=72.0873365231\ldots .
}
\tag{P.1}

Then for every fixed

\[
0<\rho<\rho_{\max}:={d_0\over\alpha}
={35739\over4519876}
=0.0079070753268\ldots,
\tag{P.2}

there are subfamilies `L_rho` of smallest-leaf outer-to-outer trains and `C_rho` of exact center-phase complete trains such that every member of `L_rho` crosses every member of `C_rho` and

\[
\boxed{
|L_\rho|\ge \rho D-o(D),
}
\tag{P.3}

\[
\boxed{
|C_\rho|>
\left(d_0-\alpha\rho\right)D-o(D).
}
\tag{P.4}

Thus the corrected rectangle theorem supplies an entire linear Pareto frontier, not only the single point `(0.003,0.35)`.

Two useful explicit consequences are:

1. a **balanced complete square**

\[
\boxed{
|L_*|>0.00779D-o(D),
\qquad
|C_*|>0.00779D-o(D);
}
\tag{P.5}

2. a **near product-optimal rectangle** with

\[
\rho_*={d_0\over2\alpha}
={35739\over9039752}
=0.0039535376634\ldots,
\]

for which

\[
\boxed{
|L_*|\ge \rho_*D-o(D),
\qquad
|C_*|>0.285D-o(D).
}
\tag{P.6}

The corresponding guaranteed complete-crossing density is

\[
\rho_*\,{57\over200}
=0.00112675823407\ldots,
\tag{P.7}

slightly above the fixed theorem's safe product `0.003*0.35=0.00105`.

---

## 1. Keep the block length symbolic

Use exactly the corrected dual-tree-backbone theorem. It produces a simple backbone path

\[
P_0=(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_s)
\]

of good smallest-leaf outer-to-outer trains with

\[
s>s_0D-o(D),
\tag{P.8}

such that every backbone leaf is crossed by more than

\[
d_0D-o(D)
\]

center trains. The total number of center trains satisfies

\[
N_C<n_0D+o(D).
\tag{P.9}

For every center train `gamma`, its intersection with `P_0` is a consecutive interval because both are paths in the same complement dual tree.

Fix a constant `rho>0` and put

\[
r=\lfloor\rho D\rfloor.
\]

When `rho<rho_max<s_0`, one has `r<s` for all sufficiently large `D`. The block-counting inequality from the parent theorem is

\[
\sum_B\#\{\gamma:B\subset N(\gamma)\cap P_0\}
\ge I_0-(r-1)N_C,
\tag{P.10}

where

\[
I_0>d_0sD-o(D^2).
\tag{P.11}

There are at most `s` consecutive `r`-blocks. Therefore some block `B_rho` has common center neighborhood of size

\[
\begin{aligned}
|N(B_\rho)|
&>{d_0sD-rN_C\over s}-o(D)\\
&>\left(d_0-{\rho n_0\over s/D}\right)D-o(D)\\
&\ge\left(d_0-{\rho n_0\over s_0}\right)D-o(D)\\
&=\left(d_0-\alpha\rho\right)D-o(D).
\end{aligned}
\tag{P.12}

Take

\[
L_\rho=B_\rho,
\qquad
C_\rho=N(B_\rho).
\]

Then every train in `L_rho` crosses every train in `C_rho`, while `|L_rho|=r=\rho D-o(D)`. This proves (P.3)--(P.4).

The upper restriction `rho<rho_max=d_0/alpha` is only the range where the displayed center lower bound remains positive. The topological extraction itself remains valid for any fixed `rho<s_0`.

---

## 2. Balanced square

The continuous balance point solves

\[
\rho=d_0-\alpha\rho,
\]

hence

\[
\rho_{bal}
={d_0\over1+\alpha}
={35739\over4582576}
=0.0077988886600\ldots .
\tag{P.13}

Choose the safely rounded value

\[
\rho={779\over100000}=0.00779.
\]

The exact center lower bound is

\[
d_0-\alpha\rho
={696271\over82500000}
=0.00843964848\ldots
>0.00779.
\tag{P.14}

Thus both shores contain more than `0.00779D-o(D)` trains, proving (P.5).

---

## 3. Product-optimal aspect ratio

The guaranteed normalized rectangle product is

\[
P(\rho)=\rho(d_0-\alpha\rho),
\]

a strictly concave quadratic on `(0,rho_max)`. Its unique maximizer is

\[
\rho_*={d_0\over2\alpha}
={35739\over9039752}
=0.0039535376634\ldots .
\tag{P.15}

At this point

\[
d_0-\alpha\rho_*={d_0\over2}={57\over200}=0.285,
\tag{P.16}

which gives (P.6)--(P.7).

For a simple decimal alternative, `rho=0.00395=79/20000` gives the exact center lower bound

\[
{89427449\over313500000}
=0.2852550207\ldots .
\]

---

## 4. Why this matters downstream

Different closure mechanisms want different rectangle aspect ratios.

- A strip-reuse argument benefits from maximizing the number of ordered outer-to-outer leaf cuts; (P.5) increases the certified leaf block from `0.003D` to `0.00779D` while retaining a linear common center family.
- A two-parameter or grid-shear argument may care about the number of complete leaf/center crossings; (P.6) maximizes the guaranteed product under the present backbone information.
- The original `0.003D x 0.35D` point remains useful when a very large common center family is the priority.

No new global-minimality conclusion follows from the Pareto optimization itself. It only exposes all of the quantitative content already latent in the corrected dual-tree rectangle proof.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only rectangle-profile sharpening; C-II and Level I remain open.
